{"id":76,"course_id":19,"title":"Game Days and Incident Readiness","summary":"Rehearse failure before the real outage writes the exam.","sequence":4,"body":"## Mental model\nGame days test people, dashboards, runbooks, automation, and recovery assumptions. They should be scoped, safe, and measured. The goal is not theater; it is finding gaps while the stakes are lower.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice designing a game day for DNS failure, bad deploy, unavailable AZ, exhausted Pod IPs, or noisy alerts. Include abort criteria and customer-safety boundaries.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nCreate an incident readiness calendar with quarterly scenarios, owners, expected evidence, and improvement backlog review.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","practice_notes":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-incident-response-reliability","course_category":"Incident Response","course_era":"Platform Academy","terms":[{"id":376,"term":"Incident Response and Reliability Leadership","context":"Incident Response","definition":"Practice incident command, severity, mitigation, communications, timelines, postmortems, corrective actions, and game days for platform roles."},{"id":377,"term":"Evidence","context":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":378,"term":"Blast radius","context":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":379,"term":"Rollback","context":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":380,"term":"Runbook","context":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":226,"lesson_id":76,"prompt":"What is the main goal of Game Days and Incident Readiness?","answer":"Rehearse failure before the real outage writes the exam.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":227,"lesson_id":76,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":228,"lesson_id":76,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
